window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][3] = "<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Apple [as of 2026-10].</strong> Apple publishes platform design guidelines and public statements on privacy <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Apple Human Interface Guidelines (developer.apple.com) and privacy pages]</code>. Third-party prep guides report team-specific loops, an emphasis on craft and ecosystem fit, and questions grounded in Apple products <code>Inferred</code> <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. In tradeoff answers, include polish and consistency as costs, not only speed and scope.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Netflix [as of 2026-10].</strong> Netflix's published culture material emphasizes context over control and individual ownership of decisions <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Netflix culture memo, June 2024]</code>. The case study CS-C02-02 is a reminder that even strong-culture companies make high-visibility decision errors; interviewers at any company like to hear what you would do differently.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Stripe [as of 2026-10].</strong> Stripe is widely described as having a strong writing culture <code>Inferred</code> <code>[V: The Pragmatic Engineer, Inside Stripe's Engineering Culture Part 2 (strong culture of writing); Slab interview with Stripe's former Documentation Manager; any claim about rigor or precise thinking is inference]</code>. Practical consequence: practice stating a problem, options and a recommendation in a few precise paragraphs, as in the decision memo (FW-C02-06), since clear reasoning on the page is a skill the company is described as valuing <code>Inferred</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c02-8-common-mistakes\"></span>\n<h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Mistake</th>\n<th>How to detect it</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Answering with a solution before naming the problem</td>\n<td>Your first sentence contains a feature</td>\n<td>State the user, the situation and the outcome first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Options with no cost</td>\n<td>You cannot finish &quot;we give up...&quot; for any option</td>\n<td>Write the sacrifice before the benefit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Treating every decision as high stakes</td>\n<td>You run a long analysis on a reversible choice</td>\n<td>Classify reversibility first (FW-C02-04)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Treating every decision as cheap</td>\n<td>You say &quot;just try it&quot; about a decision that affects customer money or data</td>\n<td>Ask who cannot easily undo it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Confidence without a basis</td>\n<td>You say &quot;I'm sure&quot; or give a precise number with no source</td>\n<td>Give a label with a reason, and say what would raise it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Letting sunk cost steer</td>\n<td>Your argument includes &quot;we've already spent...&quot;</td>\n<td>Compare only future cost and future value (see C02.5)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Writing the problem to fit the solution</td>\n<td>The problem statement uses the solution's nouns</td>\n<td>Remove the solution words and re-test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Stopping at the first-order effect</td>\n<td>You never ask &quot;and then what?&quot;</td>\n<td>Run three rounds of &quot;and then what?&quot; by stakeholder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>False either-or</td>\n<td>You accept &quot;fast or good&quot; without asking what makes fast worse</td>\n<td>Name the specific cause and test whether it can be fixed separately</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>No stated reversal condition</td>\n<td>You cannot say what would change your mind</td>\n<td>Add &quot;I would change my mind if ___&quot; to every recommendation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c02-c02-8-anti-frameworks-patterns-interviewers-discount\"></span>\n<h4>C02.8 Anti-frameworks: patterns interviewers discount</h4>\n<p>Named frameworks are fine as scaffolding. These patterns are discounted because they replace reasoning with form <code>Inferred</code>. Each is an instance of <a href=\"#gl-framework-theater\">framework theater</a> (C03).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Why it fails</th>\n<th>Do instead</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Reciting an acronym method step by step regardless of the question</td>\n<td>The structure is visible and the thinking is not</td>\n<td>Use the steps that the question needs and say why you skip others</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scoring options 1 to 10 with no basis</td>\n<td>False precision hides the real sacrifice</td>\n<td>Use ordered comparisons and say what drives the order</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The universal two-by-two</td>\n<td>The axes were chosen after the answer</td>\n<td>Choose axes from the goal, then place options</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;It depends&quot; and stop</td>\n<td>Refuses the decision</td>\n<td>Say what it depends on and then choose given your assumptions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;I'd ask the users&quot; as the whole answer</td>\n<td>Research is a step, not a decision</td>\n<td>Name what you would ask, and what you would do with each answer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Just A/B test it&quot;</td>\n<td>Not every decision can be tested, and some tests are not worth running</td>\n<td>Say what the test would decide, how long it would take, and what you do meanwhile</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Build an MVP&quot; with no riskiest assumption</td>\n<td>The label stands in for a plan</td>\n<td>Name the <a href=\"#gl-mvp\">minimum viable product</a> assumption it tests (C15)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metric lists</td>\n<td>Ten metrics are no metric</td>\n<td>Pick one primary <a href=\"#gl-metric\">metric</a>, one <a href=\"#gl-guardrail-metric\">guardrail metric</a> and say why (C05)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c02-9-mental-models-applied\"></span>\n<h3>9. Mental models applied</h3>\n<p>Four models, each tied to a product decision. Two others that this chapter's decisions touch, <a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a> and <a href=\"#gl-jobs-to-be-done\">jobs to be done</a>, are owned by C05 and C06 and linked rather than redefined.</p>\n<span id=\"c02-c02-5-four-models-in-use\"></span>\n<h4>C02.5 Four models in use</h4>\n<p><strong>Inversion</strong> (<code>MM-inversion</code>) is solving a problem by asking what would guarantee failure and then avoiding it. <a href=\"#gl-inversion\">inversion</a> works because failure modes are often easier to list than success factors. <em>Application:</em> a team designing a notification system asks &quot;how would we guarantee users mute every notification?&quot; and lists: send during sleep hours, send low-value content, send too often, make settings hard to find. The design rules are the inverse of that list. Use inversion in a pre-mortem (FW-C02-05) and in the opening of any design answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Second-order effect</strong> (<code>MM-second-order</code>) is a consequence of a consequence, usually through other people's responses. <a href=\"#gl-second-order-effect\">second-order effect</a> thinking asks &quot;and then what?&quot; for each stakeholder. <em>Application:</em> a referral bonus raises signups (first order). Bonus-seekers sign up, never become active, and push support costs up (second order). The program's unit economics decline and the team tightens the bonus, which annoys genuine referrers (third order). A bonus tied to activation avoids most of the chain (CS-C02-01).</p>\n<p><strong>Local optimum</strong> (<code>MM-local-vs-global</code>) is the best result reachable by small changes from where you are, which may be far from the best result reachable by a different approach. <a href=\"#gl-local-optimum\">local optimum</a> traps teams that tune what they have. <em>Application:</em> a signup flow improves by 2% a quarter through copy and button changes, then stalls. A step back to the <a href=\"#gl-problem-statement\">problem statement</a> shows that most visitors do not understand the product, so the next step is a different first screen rather than another copy test. Detect it with plateauing gains. Respond by reserving some effort for a different shape of solution, then evaluating it with an <a href=\"#gl-experiment\">experiment</a> when you can.</p>\n<p><strong>Sunk cost</strong> (<code>MM-sunk-cost</code>) is cost already spent and not recoverable, which should not drive a forward-looking decision. <a href=\"#gl-sunk-cost\">sunk cost</a> bias makes teams finish projects that no longer deserve finishing. <em>Application:</em> a project is 70% built and new evidence shows weak demand. The question is whether the remaining 30% of the cost buys more value than the next best use of that effort, not how much has been spent. Two cautions: option value and learning are forward-looking and can legitimately justify finishing, and a team's morale is a real cost to weigh. The model removes the past from the calculation; it does not require you to ignore the future cost of stopping.</p>\n<span id=\"c02-engineering-design-and-data-science-perspectives\"></span>\n<h3>Engineering, design and data-science perspectives</h3>\n<p>These change the decision, so they belong here.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Engineering.</strong> Reversibility looks different from the engineering side: a feature flag makes a change reversible for the code, but a data migration or a public API contract is not. Ask engineers &quot;what is the cost of undoing this, and for whom?&quot; before you classify.</li>\n<li><strong>Design.</strong> User habits are a form of state. A design change can be technically reversible and still costly to reverse, because users have relearned an interface. Ask design what the relearning cost is.</li>\n<li><strong>Data science.</strong> A decision's evidence is only as good as the question it answers. Ask what the data can and cannot tell you (observational data cannot separate cause from correlation, a point developed in C14) and what a plausible wrong answer would look like.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c02-checklist-decision-hygiene\"></span>\n<h3>Checklist: decision hygiene</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I state the decision and the goal?</li>\n<li>Did I separate the problem from the proposed solution?</li>\n<li>Did I include a smaller option and &quot;do nothing&quot;?</li>\n<li>Did I write each option's cost, and who bears it?</li>\n<li>Did I classify reversibility and the cost of delay?</li>\n<li>Did I state my confidence with a reason?</li>\n<li>Did I say what would change my mind?</li>\n<li>Did I name the first step and the earliest signal that I am wrong?</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c02-10-case-studies\"></span>\n<h3>10. Case studies</h3>\n<span id=\"cs-c02-01\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C02-01 · The referral bonus · Illustrative · Beginner</h5>\n<p><em>Fictional composite. Every number is invented.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> A budgeting app wants 50,000 new signups this quarter (invented). The growth lead proposes doubling the referral bonus from $5 to $10 per signup (invented), paid when the invited friend registers.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> The quarter ends in 10 weeks. Marketing budget allows an extra $150,000 (invented). The company's rule is that a new customer should pay back acquisition cost within 12 months.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Last quarter, referral signups were 8,000 (invented) and 35% of them were still active after 30 days (invented). Paid-channel signups had 55% 30-day activity (invented). Support contacts per referred user were 0.4 against 0.2 for paid (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> The business needs more active users, not more registrations. The proposal optimizes registrations.</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> First-order effect: doubling the bonus should raise referral signups. Second-order effects, asked stakeholder by stakeholder: bonus-seekers will register accounts they will not use, which lowers activation and raises support load; existing genuine referrers will notice the program looks transactional; and the finance rule on payback will be strained because cost per active user rises. The last effect shows up only if cost is measured per active user rather than per signup, which is the <a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a> trap (C05).</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Double the bonus on registration</td>\n<td>Signup count</td>\n<td>Lower activation, higher support, payback strain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Keep $5 on registration, add $10 when the friend completes a first budget</td>\n<td>Active users</td>\n<td>Slower payout; fewer registrations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Do nothing</td>\n<td>No spend</td>\n<td>Misses the target</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B gives up raw signup volume to protect the metric the business cares about. It is reversible: the bonus structure can be changed after two weeks with a staged rollout to half of users.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Choose B, test it on 50% of referrers for three weeks, and track cost per active user and support contacts per referred user as guardrails.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> In this fictional scenario, B produced 12% fewer referral signups than A would have (invented) but 30% more users active at 30 days per dollar (invented). The company missed the raw signup target and met the activation goal that it later adopted as the quarterly metric.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> If activation among bonus-seekers had turned out high, A would have been right, and the test would have shown it. If the finance rule had been looser, the team might have run A and B side by side. The point of the case is the method: first-order thinking would have picked A and second-order thinking changed the decision.</p>\n<span id=\"cs-c02-02\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C02-02 · Netflix, 2011: a price change and a split that were reversed · Reconstructed · Advanced</h5>\n<p><em>Facts below come from Netflix's shareholder letter for the third quarter of 2011 (filed with the US Securities and Exchange Commission) and Associated Press reports of 10 and 25 October 2011, which quote Netflix's chief executive and report the company's September guidance; the analysis is inference. No internal Netflix information is used. Re-read in October 2026.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> In July 2011 Netflix moved to charge separately for streaming and for DVDs by mail. Reporting describes the effective price of the combined plan rising from about $10 to about $16 a month, a rise of as much as 60% for some customers. In September 2011 the company announced that its DVD service would become a separate brand, Qwikster, with its own website and account. On October 10, 2011 it reversed the Qwikster plan and said customers would keep one website and one account, while keeping the July prices.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Netflix's shareholder letter says the company had failed to explain the rising cost of expanding streaming content alongside steady DVD costs, so that many customers saw it as greedy, and its chief executive described DVD-by-mail as a business in steady decline and streaming as the future. The business had millions of subscribers on a combined plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Netflix reported about 24.6 million US subscribers at the end of June 2011 and about 23.8 million at the end of September, a drop of about 800,000, against a loss of about 600,000 that the company had forecast in September. The share price fell about 35% the day after the October results and by more than half between July and October, according to Associated Press reports.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> <em>Inferred.</em> Two separate problems were bundled: the economics of content costs, which called for a price change, and the longer-term shift from DVD to streaming, which called for operational separation. The customer problem was different from both: customers valued one place to get films and one account.</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> <em>Inference.</em> Three decision-quality questions apply.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Reversibility.</em> A price increase is announced publicly and customers who leave are not guaranteed to return. The Qwikster plan was in effect a one-way announcement: reversing it was possible, but it cost credibility. The company did reverse Qwikster within weeks and kept the price change, which suggests it judged the price decision to be the necessary one.</li>\n<li><em>Second-order effects.</em> The first-order effect of the split was operational clarity. The second-order effect was that customers who liked a single account now faced two, and that the split signaled the company was moving away from the combined service they had chosen.</li>\n<li><em>Staging.</em> The decisions appear to have been announced to all customers rather than tested with a subset, which would have measured customer reaction before commitment <code>Inferred</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> <em>Reconstructed.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Price change and split, as done</td>\n<td>Economics and strategy clarity</td>\n<td>Customer trust and subscribers, as reported</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Price change only, communicate the content-cost reason</td>\n<td>Economics</td>\n<td>Some cancellations; no brand split</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Price change, then test a separate DVD brand with a subset</td>\n<td>Learning before commitment</td>\n<td>Slower strategic separation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Speed and strategic clarity were traded for customer convenience and trust, in the open, with little ability to undo the first impression.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> <em>As reported.</em> Reverse the Qwikster separation; keep the July price.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> <em>Documented through October 2011.</em> Subscriber losses and a large share-price fall, as above. Later years are outside this case.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> <em>Inference.</em> Option C might have shown the account-friction problem at small cost. A pre-mortem (FW-C02-05) would likely have listed &quot;customers do not want two accounts&quot; as a high-likelihood failure, because it is exactly the sort of reason a team imagines when told the plan failed. We cannot know whether it would have changed the decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Lesson for an interview answer.</strong> When you recommend a change that customers will see, say how reversible it is for customers, not only for engineers, and say what small test would tell you before you commit.</p>\n<span id=\"c02-11-interview-questions-and-model-answers\"></span>\n<h3>11. Interview questions and model answers</h3>\n<p>Six questions, two per difficulty. All model answers are <code>Illustrative</code>; numbers are invented. Each was written against the five signals in section 1.</p>\n<span id=\"iq-c02-01\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C02-01 · Beginner · product thinking · PS</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Users keep asking for a dark mode in your app. Would you build it?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, users are asking for it, and many apps have it.&quot; No segment, no cost, no measure.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks who is asking and why, estimates demand from available data, names the cost, compares with alternatives and decides with a criterion.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates a preference from a need, finds the smallest reversible step, defines a metric and a counter-metric, and says what would change the answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I would start by restating the need behind the request. People who ask for dark mode are usually doing one of three things: reading at night and wanting less glare, saving battery on some displays, or needing higher contrast for accessibility. Those are different problems, and the third is a need rather than a preference.</p>\n<p>Next I would check the evidence we already have. How many of the requests mention night use, and from which segment? What share of sessions happen between 9pm and 6am? Request counts also overstate demand, because the people who ask are the people who care most, so I would pair them with usage data rather than rely on the count alone.</p>\n<p>Options of different kinds: follow the phone's system theme setting, which is the smallest option and covers a large share of the need; build an in-app toggle with its own theme; do nothing and invest the effort elsewhere. The system-theme option costs the least and is easy to reverse. A full custom theme costs more design and testing time, and it takes engineering effort away from the work that is competing for it.</p>\n<p>My recommendation is to follow the system setting first, because it serves most of the need at a fraction of the cost, and to expand to a toggle only if users with the system setting turned on still ask for more control. I would measure how many users have the new theme active and whether night-time session length changes for them, with crash rate and support contacts as guardrails, and the share of users who switch the new theme off within a week as the counter-metric. I would change my mind if the requests cluster in an accessibility segment: contrast then becomes a quality requirement rather than a nice-to-have.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if most of the requests come from users who already have their phone set to dark? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then the request is probably about control, not about the theme, because those users already get a dark appearance in other apps. That makes an in-app toggle more plausible. I would check what share of users override the system setting in other apps and ask five of the requesters what they want to control. Once a theme system exists, a toggle adds little cost, so the decision becomes cheap; I would still ship it behind the same guardrails.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you measure success when dark mode barely moves revenue? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would not expect revenue to move, so I would measure the jobs it serves: adoption among night-time users, night-time session length or task completion for them, and support contacts or reviews that mention glare or contrast. I would test retention against a holdout and treat any gain as a bonus, not a promise. If only satisfaction and accessibility complaints improve, that is a legitimate result, and I would describe it as a quality investment and not a growth lever.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c02-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C02-02 · Beginner · prioritization · EX</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Sales asks you to build a custom report for one large customer who is threatening to leave. Do you build it?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, the customer is important&quot; or &quot;No, we never build one-offs,&quot; with no reasoning.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Sizes the customer's value, checks whether the need is shared, scopes the smallest version, names the cost to the roadmap, and decides by a stated criterion.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates the request from the underlying job, tests whether a configurable version serves others, time-boxes the work, names the precedent cost, and says what would change the answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I would not answer yes or no yet. I would ask what the customer is trying to do with the report, because &quot;a custom report&quot; is one way to meet a need, and the need may be shared by other customers.</p>\n<p>Then I would size three things. First, the customer's value: say the account is 6% of annual revenue and renews in three months (invented). Second, how many other customers have asked for something similar, from support and sales notes. Third, the cost: two engineers for three weeks (invented), which is work that would otherwise go to the roadmap.</p>\n<p>My options differ in kind. Build exactly what was asked, gaining the account and giving up three weeks of roadmap plus a precedent that large customers get one-offs. Build a configurable export or report builder that covers this case and others, which costs more now and serves more customers. Offer an interim workaround, such as a data export and a template the customer's analyst can use this week, which is cheap and reversible.</p>\n<p>I recommend the workaround this week, to remove the immediate threat, and a time-boxed scoping of the configurable version, with the decision based on how many other customers show the same need. The cost is that the customer gets less than they asked for at first, and I would explain why and when they get more. I would change the recommendation if the contract names the report, if the account is large enough that losing it changes the company's plans, or if scoping shows the configurable version costs about the same as the one-off.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Sales says the customer will leave on Friday unless you commit. What do you say? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would ask sales what the customer actually said and what would satisfy them this week. A dated commitment to a defined interim (an export and a template) plus an executive call often does. I would not promise the full report under a deadline I cannot verify; I would state what I can deliver and by when, in writing. If the customer truly requires the full report, I would take it to my manager with the revenue at stake and the cost, because it is then a leadership tradeoff about the roadmap, not mine alone.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you stop this from becoming a precedent where every large customer gets a one-off? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;With a written rule applied consistently: custom work is considered when the need is shared by several accounts or the account passes a revenue threshold (say 5% of revenue, invented), it is time-boxed, and it is built as a configurable capability where possible. I would count such requests and their cost each quarter. Sales also gets a clear path, so they can say 'not as a one-off, but here is how it can be considered' instead of promising on the spot.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c02-03\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C02-03 · Intermediate · execution · EX</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Engineering proposes removing a feature that is rarely used but that a few large enterprise customers rely on. How do you decide?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Counts users and removes it because &quot;usage is low,&quot; or keeps it because &quot;customers might leave.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Measures revenue and strategic weight of the affected customers, estimates the carrying cost, considers alternatives, and plans the removal.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Classifies the decision as one-way for the customers, reduces the irreversibility with a notice period and a migration path, and states the conditions that would make keeping it right.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> Usage counts are the wrong first measure: a feature used by 2% of accounts can be used by 20% of revenue. So I would ask four questions. Which customers use it, and what share of revenue and renewals do they represent (invented example: 5 accounts, 9% of annual revenue)? What does it cost to keep, in engineering time, incident risk and the drag it adds to other changes? What do the customers do with it, and is there an alternative path? And what is the decision's reversibility?</p>\n<p>For the customers, removal is a one-way door. They have built workflows on it. That points to the mode &quot;decide with evidence and reduce the irreversibility,&quot; not &quot;decide quickly.&quot;</p>\n<p>My options: keep it as is; keep it but freeze it, with no new investment; replace it with a supported alternative and migrate customers; remove it with a long notice period. Keeping it as is gives up engineering capacity. Removal gives up the revenue risk and the trust of the customers, whose renewals may be tied to the feature. Replacing it asks us to build something, and costs the most effort but is the only option that serves both sides.</p>\n<p>I would recommend freezing it now, investigating a replacement path with the five customers, and deciding on removal when we know the replacement cost and each customer's migration effort. If the carrying cost turns out to be large and the replacement path is cheap, I would remove it with at least a quarter's notice and a named migration contact. I would change my mind if the customers' contracts name the feature or if the replacement cost exceeds the revenue at risk.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> The five customers say they will never migrate. Do you still remove the feature? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;'Never' under pressure often means 'not for free', so I would ask what migration would take and what we could offer: tooling, a named migration contact, a longer notice, perhaps a price adjustment. Then I would compare the carrying cost with the revenue at risk (9% of annual revenue in the example, invented) after expected churn, with finance. If the revenue at risk is larger than the savings, I keep it frozen. If it blocks other work and the replacement path is cheap, I remove it with a long notice. Either way it is a business decision I would bring to leadership with the numbers.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Engineering says the feature causes most of the team's incidents. How does that change your decision? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;It turns the carrying cost from abstract into measurable: incidents per month, engineer-hours on call, and the delay to other work. If, say, 40% of on-call load comes from this feature (invented), freezing is not enough. I would choose between hardening it, which is cheaper and keeps it, and building the replacement path, using the same customer conversations. Doing nothing has a cost too, and I would put that number next to the revenue at risk.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c02-04\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C02-04 · Intermediate · product thinking · PR</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Customer support costs have doubled in a year, while the customer base grew 30%. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Add a chatbot&quot; or &quot;hire more efficient agents,&quot; with no diagnosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Decomposes cost into volume and cost per contact, finds the drivers, and addresses the largest.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates fixed facts from conventions, computes the per-customer change, finds root causes upstream of support, and chooses between deflecting contacts and removing the reason for them.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I would start with arithmetic, because &quot;doubled&quot; and &quot;grew 30%&quot; are not the same thing. If total cost doubled and customers grew 1.3 times, cost per customer rose by about 54% (2 divided by 1.3, which is 1.54). So growth explains part of the increase, but not most of it.</p>\n<p>Decompose cost. Cost equals contacts times cost per contact. Contacts equal customers times contact rate. Cost per contact equals handling time times labor cost per minute plus tooling. I would find which factor moved: did the contact rate rise, did handling time lengthen, or did the labor cost change? Suppose the data (invented) show the contact rate per customer up 40%, handling time up 10%, and the rest from labor.</p>\n<p>The contact rate is the largest driver, so I would examine its reasons. Break contacts by reason and by customer cohort: billing questions, delivery issues, product defects, &quot;how do I&quot; questions. Say billing questions are 35% of the increase (invented). The first-principles question is: what is the user trying to do, and why can they not do it without help? If the billing page is confusing, the fix is a clearer page, which removes contacts rather than handling them.</p>\n<p>The options differ in kind. A chatbot deflects contacts at the support layer, is quick to build, and leaves the root cause. Improving the billing page removes contacts at the source, takes longer, and benefits customers who never contacted support. Training agents reduces handling time and affects the smaller factor. I would recommend working on the top two contact reasons at the source, with a limited deflection tool for the long tail. I would measure contacts per customer by reason, not total cost, with customer satisfaction as the <a href=\"#gl-guardrail-metric\">guardrail metric</a> so that cost cuts do not come from worse service. I would change course if the top reasons turn out to be outside our control, such as carrier delays.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Billing questions are the largest driver of the extra contacts. What do you do first? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would split billing contacts by cause: invoice confusion, failed payments, refunds, plan changes. I would fix the most frequent cause at the source, for example a clearer invoice and a self-serve refund path, and measure contacts per 1,000 customers by reason before and after. I would also check whether contacts are first contacts or repeats. Failed payments are worth a look because they create both contacts and cancellations, so better retries and notifications may cut both at once.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Leadership wants a chatbot now. Do you build it? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Not as the first step. A bot deflects some contacts and leaves the cause, and a bad one raises repeat contacts and frustration. I would fix the top two root causes first, then use a bot or help content only for questions that stay high-volume and answerable. I would measure deflection honestly: resolved with no repeat contact within seven days, plus satisfaction. If the resolution rate is under about 40% (invented) or repeat contacts rise, I would stop and say why.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c02-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C02-05 · Hard · product thinking · PS</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Your VP asks the team to grow daily active users by 20% this quarter. How do you respond?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Run growth campaigns and add notifications,&quot; with no discussion of the target.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks what the target is for, decomposes the metric, proposes options and guardrails.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Anticipates how the metric will be gamed, separates real value from activity, proposes counter-metrics and staged tests, and negotiates the target with a reason.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I would first ask what the target is meant to achieve, because daily active users is a proxy. If the underlying goal is more people getting value, then there are targets that measure it more directly, and I would raise that with the VP rather than silently optimize a proxy. If the VP has a reason for this one, such as a board commitment, I would take it as given and work within it.</p>\n<p>Decompose the metric. Daily active users equal new users plus returning users, minus those who stop. I would look at what drives each, and where the cheapest honest gain lies: activation of new users, resurrection of lapsed ones, or frequency among those who already use the product.</p>\n<p>Then I would think through second-order effects, because this target invites gaming. Notifications can raise daily activity by prompting opens that deliver no value, which increases the count and erodes trust and opt-in rates later. That is a classic <a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a> outcome. So I would pair the target with counter-metrics: notification opt-out rate, sessions with a meaningful action, and 30-day retention.</p>\n<p>My plan would test two or three levers in order of honesty about value: a better first-week experience, a reminder tied to something the user said they care about, and a content improvement. Each is tested as an <a href=\"#gl-experiment\">experiment</a> with the counter-metrics watched. I would tell the VP up front what I expect: a gain of a certain size from the honest levers (a range, with low confidence until we have a first read), and what extra gain would come only from tactics I would not recommend. If the full 20% is reachable only by tactics that damage retention, I would propose a smaller target for DAU with a retention floor. I would change my plan if the first experiments show frequency, not activation, is where the headroom is.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the VP says the number is fixed: 20% and nothing else? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would accept the number and protect what it stands for. I would propose the levers (activation, resurrection, frequency), the counter-metrics and a forecast range with my confidence, and ask that the counter-metrics be reported alongside the target. I would also say which tactics I will not use, such as notifications that deliver no value. If it is a board commitment, I would ask what the board cares about, since a related measure may be acceptable, but I would not reopen the question repeatedly.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Which lever would you try first, and how would you know the growth is honest? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;The first-week experience, because new-user activation compounds and is least likely to be gaming. Honest growth shows in cohorts: new users activating and returning at the baseline rate or better, sessions with a meaningful action rising with total sessions, opt-outs and complaints flat, and 30-day retention of the added users similar to older cohorts. If the extra daily users have short sessions and high opt-outs, the number is inflated.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c02-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C02-06 · Hard · behavioral · BE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Tell me about a decision you made with incomplete information.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> A story with no stated options, no stated information gaps, and an outcome that is the whole point.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the options, the missing information, the decision rule and the result, and says what was learned.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows how the candidate sized the cost of being wrong, made the decision reversible, set a trigger to revisit it, and separates decision quality from the outcome.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <em>Illustrative: a composite story, numbers invented.</em> &quot;I was the PM for a scheduling feature at a software company. Two weeks before a planned release, a customer-facing team told me that a partner calendar integration might behave differently in time zones than we had tested. We could not reproduce it, and the partner's documentation was unclear. I had three options: delay the release by three weeks to get the partner's confirmation, release with the integration enabled for everyone, or release without the integration and add it later.</p>\n<p>&quot;I defined the cost of being wrong in each case. A wrong meeting time is more than an annoyance, because someone misses a meeting. That made the integration the risky part, and the rest of the release was fine. A delay would have cost three weeks on a feature that customers had asked for. Releasing the rest without the integration would give up about a third of the expected use but carried no risk of wrong times.</p>\n<p>&quot;I chose to release without the integration, put the integration in a small beta with 30 volunteer customers (invented), and set a rule: expand when we saw no time-zone errors across 500 scheduled meetings (invented), and pause if one was reported. I told my manager at the time that I was about 60% confident the integration was fine and that this was a judgment, not a measurement.</p>\n<p>&quot;The beta found one edge case with a half-hour time zone offset. We fixed it and expanded the integration five weeks later. I would make the same decision again. If the problem had not appeared, I would still have given up about five weeks of integration use, which I counted as a known cost. What I changed afterward was to ask partners for sandbox environments early in the project, because the information gap was avoidable.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What would you have done if the beta had found several time-zone errors? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;The rule I set in advance was to pause on one reported error, so I would have paused the integration for everyone outside the beta, found the cause with the partner and told the beta customers what happened. The release without the integration was already out, so the exposure was limited to 30 customers. I would have repeated the beta after a fix under the same rule, and reconsidered whether the integration was worth shipping at all if errors persisted.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> You told your manager you were about 60% confident. Why say that out loud? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Stating it turns a feeling into something my manager can challenge or accept. It shows I know the decision is a judgment, and it gives me a way to check my calibration later: across many decisions, were my 60s right about 60% of the time? It also changes how the team prepares: at 60% they want the beta and a rollback; at 95% they would just ship.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c02-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Ten exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Numbers in scenarios are invented.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c02-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C02-01 · Reframe three requests</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Rewrite each request as a need, a segment and an outcome: (1) &quot;Add a search bar to the dashboard.&quot; (2) &quot;Make onboarding shorter.&quot; (3) &quot;We need a mobile app.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> You are a PM at a B2B analytics product. Requests came from sales, an executive and a customer.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Support logs show 18% of tickets are &quot;can't find report&quot; (invented). Onboarding completion is 52% (invented). 38% of sessions are on mobile browsers (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> You have 20 minutes and no new research budget.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> For each, apply the product thinking chain: who, what is happening, why it matters, evidence, and what is not the problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (1) Need: users who cannot locate a saved report. Segment: occasional users with many reports. Outcome: fewer &quot;can't find report&quot; tickets and faster time-to-report. Not the problem: the search bar itself, since a naming or navigation change may do it. (2) Need: new users reach first value. Segment: users who drop before completing setup.</p>\n<p>Outcome: raise completion from 52%. Not the problem: the number of steps; fewer steps may not help if one step is the barrier. (3) Need: users on phones cannot do something they need. Segment: the 38% on mobile browsers. Outcome: task completion on mobile. Not the problem: &quot;an app&quot;; a responsive web view might serve the need.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Write each as a <a href=\"#gl-problem-statement\">problem statement</a> using the five-row template and share it with the requester to check you captured their need.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Time spent reframing delays the responses to the requesters; a quick restatement back to them preserves the relationship while you check.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c02-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C02-02 · First-principles cost decomposition</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A food-delivery startup charges $3.50 per delivery and loses money. Decompose the cost of one delivery from first principles and identify what is fixed and what is convention.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Average delivery is 3 km; couriers are paid per delivery.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Courier pay $2.80 per delivery (invented), payment processing 2.5% of a $20 order (invented), support $0.30 per order (invented), insurance $0.15 per order (invented), average courier waits 6 minutes at the restaurant (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> You cannot change the restaurant's prep time.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> List the cost components, label each as fixed floor or convention, then rebuild options.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Cost per order: courier $2.80, processing $0.50, support $0.30, insurance $0.15, which totals $3.75, against $3.50 charged. Fixed floors: some courier pay (distance and time are real), processing fees (set by networks), insurance. Conventions: paying per delivery regardless of batching, charging a flat fee regardless of distance, a six-minute wait at the restaurant. Options: batch two deliveries per trip, which cuts courier pay per order; distance-based fees; encourage restaurants to signal ready times. Batching changes the largest component and is the first one to test.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Raise the fee to $4.00. It fixes the arithmetic and gives up conversion; test it as an <a href=\"#gl-experiment\">experiment</a> before committing.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Batching reduces cost and lengthens delivery times for some customers.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c02-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C02-03 · The tradeoff table</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Build a tradeoff table for this decision: a photo-sharing app can add video uploads in six weeks, add better photo editing in four weeks, or improve upload reliability in three weeks.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The goal this quarter is retention of existing users. Team of five engineers.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Uploads fail for 6% of attempts (invented). 22% of active users edit photos weekly (invented). Competitor apps support video.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Only one of the three is possible this quarter.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Fill the table (optimizes, costs, reversibility, evidence needed) and write the recommendation line.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Upload reliability: optimizes retention among frustrated users, costs the chance to match a competitor, fully reversible, evidence available now (failure rate by cohort). Photo editing: optimizes engagement for a quarter of weekly users, costs reliability work, reversible. Video: optimizes competitive parity, costs the longest build and the highest risk, hard to reverse because storage and moderation costs commit the company. Recommendation: &quot;Fix upload reliability, which protects retention by giving up the video parity move this quarter; reversible; I would switch to editing if failure rates are below 2% after the fix.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Start video as a limited beta in parallel using one engineer, accepting a slower reliability fix.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Parity pressure is real, so the recommendation must say what would pull video forward.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c02-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C02-04 · Classify five decisions</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Classify each decision using FW-C02-04: (a) change an email subject line; (b) raise subscription price by 10% for existing users; (c) migrate user data to a new database; (d) rename a menu item; (e) shut down a free tier.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> You are the PM of a subscription product.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None beyond the descriptions.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Write one sentence of reasoning each.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> For each, ask: reversible, error costly, waiting costly; then pick a mode.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Reversible, low error cost: decide now. (b) Hard to reverse once users have seen it; high error cost; waiting cost depends on margin: stage by cohort and communicate. (c) Reversible only with a backout plan; high error cost: decide with evidence and a rehearsed rollback. (d) Reversible: decide now. (e) Hard to reverse, high error cost: decide with evidence, give notice, offer a path.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add the audience dimension: reversibility for users, for engineering and for the brand can differ.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Over-classifying small decisions wastes time; the exercise is to be quick on (a) and (d).</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c02-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C02-05 · Inversion drill</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Use inversion to design the first-run experience of a language-learning app. List ten ways to guarantee users never complete a first lesson, then invert them.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Users arrive from an ad; many are casual.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> 60% of installs never open a lesson (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Ten minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> List failure causes, then derive design rules.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Failures: require account creation first; ask for notification permission immediately; show a long placement test; hide the first lesson; use jargon; require a payment method; load slowly; show an unclear goal; ask personal questions; show an error on first tap. Inverted rules: let users try a lesson before signing up; ask for permissions after value; use a short placement; make the first lesson one tap; plain language; no payment until after value; fast loading; clear goal; ask only what you need; test first-tap paths.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Run the same exercise with the team as a pre-mortem before the next release.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Skipping account creation reduces data capture; the cost is that you learn less about trial users until later.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c02-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C02-06 · Second-order effects of a feature</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A ride-hailing app adds a &quot;tip your driver&quot; prompt at ride end. Map first-, second- and third-order effects for riders, drivers and the company.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The app currently has no in-app tipping.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Include at least one effect you would measure with a counter-metric.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Ask &quot;and then what?&quot; three times per stakeholder.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Riders: first, some tip; second, others feel social pressure and rate drivers lower or avoid the prompt; third, rider satisfaction and rating accuracy shift. Drivers: first, extra income; second, drivers favor rides likely to tip; third, service inequality across neighborhoods. Company: first, driver satisfaction rises; second, pressure to cut fares or fees may follow; third, regulators may ask about earnings. Counter-metrics: ride-cancellation rate by area, rider rating distribution, tip rate by area.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Test the prompt in two cities with different norms before a wider launch.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> The prompt helps drivers and risks rider goodwill; the choice is where to place it (end of ride versus the receipt).</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c02-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C02-07 · Escaping a local optimum</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> An online store's checkout conversion has improved 1% a quarter for a year and now plateaus at 62% (invented). Diagnose the plateau and propose a step outside the current approach.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Tests so far: button copy, field order, trust badges.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> 55% of cart abandoners say &quot;just browsing&quot; in exit surveys (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Engineering capacity is limited to one large project.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Ask what the incremental tests assume and what a different shape of solution would change.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> The tests assume the problem lives inside checkout. Exit surveys suggest many abandoners were never ready to buy, so the lever is upstream: saved carts, price-drop reminders, or comparison tools. A larger project is a &quot;save for later&quot; flow that returns people when ready. Test it as an <a href=\"#gl-experiment\">experiment</a> on carts above a certain value.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Keep optimizing checkout for the 45% who say they intended to buy.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A new flow takes longer and may not move the headline conversion rate, since its users were counted as abandoners before.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c02-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C02-08 · Sunk cost</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A project to build an in-app calendar is 70% complete after four months. New research shows most users prefer to sync with their existing calendar. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Four engineers have worked on it; three more months remain.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Interviews with 15 users (invented): 12 prefer syncing, 3 want a built-in calendar.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> The team will ask you to finish &quot;since we are almost done.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Ignore costs spent. Compare the remaining cost and value of finishing with the best alternative, including partial reuse.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> The remaining three months should compete with a sync integration. Check what part of the calendar work is reusable for sync (date logic, event model). If the integration reuses 40% of it, finishing the calendar is not the default. Recommend pivoting the remaining effort to sync, and keep the three users who want a built-in calendar in a follow-up interview.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Finish a minimal calendar if learning shows that it is a differentiator for a segment you value.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Stopping costs morale and the sense of finishing; set a clear message about what the team learned and what will ship.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c02-09\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C02-09 · Write a one-page decision memo</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Write a decision memo for: a news app must decide whether to add a paywall after five free articles a month, or keep an ad-only model.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Ad revenue per user has fallen 15% in a year (invented). The CEO wants a recommendation in a week.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> 4% of monthly readers read more than five articles (invented). 1.2% of readers have ever subscribed to anything in the app category (invented). Ad revenue is 90% of income.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> One page; include reversibility and what would change the recommendation.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use FW-C02-06.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Decision: whether to meter after five free articles. Recommendation: pilot a meter at eight articles for 10% of readers for six weeks, which gives up some ad impressions and tests willingness to pay with limited downside. Context: ad revenue is falling and the meter touches only 4% of readers. Options: full paywall, meter pilot, ad-only with more ad load, do nothing. Criteria: revenue, reader trust, reversibility. Reversibility: a meter can be loosened, but readers who leave may not return. Would change: if conversion in the pilot is below the level needed to offset lost ad revenue, shift to improving ad yield.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Run a survey of readers on willingness to pay; weaker evidence because of the <a href=\"#gl-say-do-gap\">say-do gap</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A pilot is slower than a full launch and costs some ad revenue.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c02-10\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C02-10 · Run a pre-mortem</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Run a pre-mortem on launching a &quot;buy now, pay later&quot; option in a shopping app.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Launch in eight weeks with a lending partner.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Produce a ranked list, mitigations and at least one change to the launch plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Declare failure, list reasons independently, cluster, rank, assign mitigations and owners.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Clusters: customers over-borrow and default (high impact), partner declines too many applicants (medium), checkout friction raises abandonment (medium), regulatory disclosure gaps (high impact, <code>[varies by market]</code>), support cannot handle disputes (medium). Mitigations: clear limits and repayment reminders, partner approval-rate monitoring, a one-tap checkout test, legal review of disclosures, a dispute path with a named owner. Change to plan: launch to a small share of customers with a hold on expansion if default and complaint rates exceed set thresholds.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Run the pre-mortem separately with the partner and engineering, then merge.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A cautious launch reduces learning speed and protects customers.</p>\n<span id=\"c02-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drill (twice a week for three weeks).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Pick a prompt: a tradeoff, a &quot;should we build it&quot; or a &quot;why that decision&quot; question (use IQ-C02-01 to IQ-C02-06 and the exercises above).</li>\n<li>Set a timer for ten minutes. Speak aloud or write the answer in the Type A structure from section 4.</li>\n<li>Record yourself. At minute ten, stop.</li>\n<li>Score with the checklist below.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (score each item yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I state the decision and the goal before options?</li>\n<li>Did I separate the problem from the solution?</li>\n<li>Did I include a smaller option and &quot;do nothing&quot;?</li>\n<li>Did I name each option's cost in a sacrifice sentence?</li>\n<li>Did I say who bears the cost?</li>\n<li>Did I classify reversibility and the cost of delay?</li>\n<li>Did I state confidence with a reason?</li>\n<li>Did I say what would change my mind?</li>\n<li>Did I end with a recommendation in one sentence?</li>\n<li>Did I stay within ten minutes?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to interrupt with &quot;why not the other option?&quot; at minute five. Note whether your second response is reasoning or defence.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> For each drill, record the date, the prompt, your score on the ten items, the one item you will fix next time, and the sentence you wish you had said. After six drills, look for the item you miss most often and drill that one.</p>\n<span id=\"c02-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Apply the chapter at your current job in the next two weeks. Each deliverable doubles as an interview story.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> List your last five meaningful decisions at work. Classify each using FW-C02-04, and note whether you gave it the right speed. Keep the two that show a clear tradeoff.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Take one live feature request and run it through the product thinking chain (FW-C02-01). Write the problem using the five-row template. Share it with the requester and note whether they agree it captured the need.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Write one decision memo (FW-C02-06) for a real decision that is about to be made, even if you are not the decision owner. Ask your manager for feedback on the &quot;what would change this&quot; line.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Run a pre-mortem (FW-C02-05) on your next launch with the team. Bring a list of changes you propose, and an owner for each.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Start a prediction log: before the next experiment readout or launch, write your prediction and confidence. Score it afterward.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become stories:</strong> the decision memo (a decision made with incomplete information), the pre-mortem (a risk you caught early), and the reframed request (customer or problem focus). Keep the numbers real; do not inflate them.</p>\n<span id=\"c02-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c02-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Start from the user's problem and the outcome you can measure, not from the proposed solution, unless the decision is cheap and reversible.</li>\n<li>Put the sacrifice in the sentence: every option has a cost, and a choice without one is unfinished.</li>\n<li>Classify reversibility, error cost and cost of delay before you decide how much evidence to demand.</li>\n<li>Ask &quot;and then what?&quot; for each stakeholder, and ask what would guarantee failure, before you commit.</li>\n<li>Close every recommendation with what would change your mind.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c02-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Donald G. Reinertsen, <em>The Principles of Product Development Flow</em> (2009). The economic framing of decisions, including cost of delay.</li>\n<li>Gary Klein, &quot;Performing a Project Premortem,&quot; <em>Harvard Business Review</em>, September 2007. The source of the pre-mortem technique in FW-C02-05.</li>\n<li>Daniel Kahneman, <em>Thinking, Fast and Slow</em> (2011). A broad account of judgment biases, including overconfidence and the sunk-cost fallacy.</li>\n<li>Annie Duke, <em>Thinking in Bets</em> (2018). Separating decision quality from outcome, and stating confidence.</li>\n<li>Marty Cagan, <em>Inspired</em> (2nd edition, 2017). Product discovery as a problem-first discipline.</li>\n<li>Jeff Bezos, Amazon shareholder letters for 2015 (published April 2016) and 2016, which describe one-way (Type 1) and two-way (Type 2) door decisions; the letters are on Amazon's investor relations site <code>[V: letters as quoted in AWS and secondary material; the letters themselves were not opened]</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c03\"></span>\n<h2>C03 — Structured Problem Solving: MECE, Issue Trees, Hypotheses</h2>\n<span id=\"c03-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>The skill is turning an unclear question into parts that can be examined, ranked and answered in the time available, and then reasoning from a stated guess to a test to a conclusion. C02 trained you to reach a decision and show its cost. This chapter trains the step before it: finding out what you are deciding about.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code> from the round structures candidates commonly report: the analytical or execution round (&quot;a metric dropped, why?&quot;, &quot;why did usage fall after launch?&quot;), the strategy or business round (&quot;should the company enter this market?&quot;), the first minutes of an estimation prompt (decomposition is structure, and C04 builds on this chapter), and the user-segmentation step of any product design answer (C07). On the job the same skill appears in incident reviews, in a vague executive ask like &quot;engagement feels off,&quot; in a metric dip you have to explain before Friday, and in backlog debates where nobody agrees what the problem is.</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> Google publicly describes hiring on general cognitive ability, role-related knowledge, leadership and &quot;Googleyness&quot; <code>Public</code> <code>[V: summaries citing Google's how-we-hire page; Google re:Work structured-interviewing guide]</code>, and a structured analytical round is the usual place the first is observed <code>Inferred</code>. Amazon publishes principles that include Dive Deep and Insist on the Highest Standards <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Amazon's Leadership Principles page, amazon.jobs]</code>, which in practice rewards candidates who separate symptom from cause and check their reasoning <code>Inferred</code>. The scorer's note tends to read &quot;clear structure, prioritized, reached a conclusion&quot; or &quot;listed possibilities, did not narrow&quot; <code>Inferred</code>.</p>\n<p>Five observable behaviors, which the rest of the chapter trains:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You clarify the question and the decision before you structure.</li>\n<li>Your structure is <a href=\"#gl-mece\">MECE</a> along a cut that serves the decision.</li>\n<li>You prioritize branches and say why.</li>\n<li>You state hypotheses with the evidence that would reject them.</li>\n<li>You finish with a conclusion or next action, and you separate a symptom from its cause.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Four patterns cost candidates and new PMs credibility: brainstorming a long list of &quot;possible reasons&quot; with no order, reciting a named framework instead of thinking (<a href=\"#gl-framework-theater\">framework theater</a>, defined in C03.7), boiling the ocean (an exhaustive tree with no priority, so no answer), and anchoring on the first idea and then collecting evidence for it.</p>\n<span id=\"c03-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<span id=\"c03-c03-1-clarify-and-frame-turning-a-vague-prompt-into-a-questi\"></span>\n<h4>C03.1 Clarify and frame: turning a vague prompt into a question</h4>\n<p>&quot;Engagement is down&quot; is a statement, not a question. Before you can structure anything you need a question with a decision attached. Framing has three jobs.</p>\n<p><strong>Pin the phenomenon.</strong> What exactly changed? Which <a href=\"#gl-metric\">metric</a>, defined how, by how much, since when, and was the change sudden or gradual? A drop in &quot;engagement&quot; can mean daily users, sessions per user or time in app, and each points at different causes.</p>\n<p><strong>Pin the decision.</strong> What would we do differently depending on the answer? If every plausible answer leads to the same action, the analysis is decoration. If the VP will only fund a fix on the checkout page, a diagnosis that ends at &quot;users are less engaged&quot; does not help.</p>\n<p><strong>Pin the scope.</strong> Which users, platforms, regions and time window; which constraints on time and people; and what has already happened (a release, a campaign, an outage, a holiday).</p>\n<p>Good clarifying questions fall into five kinds:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>Why it changes the structure</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Definition</td>\n<td>&quot;How is a session defined?&quot;</td>\n<td>Different definitions imply different causes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Magnitude and timing</td>\n<td>&quot;How large, since when, sudden or gradual?&quot;</td>\n<td>Sudden points to an event; gradual to a trend</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scope</td>\n<td>&quot;All platforms or one? New users or existing?&quot;</td>\n<td>Narrows the tree immediately</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Context</td>\n<td>&quot;Anything launched or changed in that window?&quot;</td>\n<td>The cheapest hypothesis is often the last release</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Decision</td>\n<td>&quot;Who acts on this, and what can they change?&quot;</td>\n<td>Fixes which branches are worth examining</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Ask three to five, in about two minutes, choosing the ones whose answers would change your structure. When the interviewer says &quot;you decide,&quot; state the assumption aloud and continue: &quot;I'll assume the drop is about 8% over two weeks on all platforms; tell me if that's wrong.&quot; The frame card (FW-C03-01) records the result.</p>\n<p>Why it works: the structure you build depends on the question you ask. A tree built for the wrong question produces branches that are neither wrong nor useful.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to use the full routine.</strong> If the prompt is already specific and bounded, ask one confirming question and move on. An estimation prompt with clear terms, or a follow-up inside an answer you already framed, does not need a new round of clarification.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> A candidate asks eight clarifying questions in a ten-minute segment, spends six minutes on them, and never structures. The interviewer sees thoroughness without progress. Clarification has diminishing returns: after the three or four questions that could change your structure, the next question is stalling.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Hidden risk.</strong> Clarifying questions can be used to ask the interviewer to do your thinking (&quot;what do you think caused it?&quot;). Ask for facts and scope, not for the answer.</p>\n<span id=\"c03-c03-2-mece-the-principle-bad-versus-good-choosing-the-cut\"></span>\n<h4>C03.2 MECE: the principle, bad versus good, choosing the cut</h4>\n<p><strong>MECE</strong> (mutually exclusive, collectively exhaustive) is a way of splitting a problem into parts that do not overlap and together cover the whole. <a href=\"#gl-mece\">MECE</a> works because each failure of it has a cost. Overlap means you count or discuss the same thing twice and cannot tell which branch explains the result. A gap means the real cause may sit in a branch you never opened.</p>\n<p>A bad split and a good one:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Whole</th>\n<th>Bad split</th>\n<th>Why it fails</th>\n<th>Good split</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Users who did not sign up</td>\n<td>Mobile, iOS, new users</td>\n<td>Overlaps (iOS is mobile; new users use both) and has gaps (web, returning users)</td>\n<td>Platform: iOS, Android, web</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Revenue fell</td>\n<td>Marketing problem, product problem, bad luck</td>\n<td>Mixes functions with a vague residual</td>\n<td>Volume, conversion, price per order</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reasons for churn</td>\n<td>Price, quality, competitors, other</td>\n<td>&quot;Other&quot; is likely the largest bucket; &quot;competitors&quot; overlaps price and quality</td>\n<td>Involuntary (payment failed); voluntary because the need is gone; voluntary because they still have the need and use something else</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>The cut is a choice.</strong> The same whole can be split in many MECE ways, and the right one is the cut that serves the decision. The test is: &quot;If the answer is branch A rather than branch B, would I do something different?&quot; Revenue can be cut by customer segment, by channel, by product line, or as volume × price. If the decision is where to send the growth team, cut by channel. If the decision is whether to change the price, cut volume from price. A MECE cut that leads to the same action in every branch is correct and useless.</p>\n<p><strong>Mathematical decompositions are MECE by construction.</strong> Revenue = visitors × conversion × order value. Every change in revenue maps to a change in some factor, and the factors do not overlap. For quantitative problems this is often the best first cut, and it is the basis for the metric trees in C05.</p>\n<p><strong>Common splits for product problems:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>By time: before and after an event; trend versus step.</li>\n<li>By funnel step: each user leaves at one step.</li>\n<li>By user attribute: new and returning; platform; region; acquisition source.</li>\n<li>By cause type: internal and external; product, data, market.</li>\n<li>By input: volume, rate, value.</li>\n<li>By participant: supply and demand; buyer and seller; user and advertiser.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Perfect MECE is rarely achievable and need not be. Aim for MECE enough: no important overlaps, gaps acknowledged out loud (&quot;this covers the main causes; I'm leaving out regulatory change as unlikely&quot;), and any &quot;other&quot; bucket small. A rule of thumb is that an &quot;other&quot; holding more than about a fifth of the whole means a branch is missing.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to use it.</strong> At the start of ideation, forcing MECE can suppress the odd-but-important idea. List freely first, then cluster.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> A team structures user pain points into four tidy categories before interviewing anyone, then finds the most severe problem (a trust issue after a billing mistake) fits none of them and is forced into &quot;other.&quot; For divergent work such as generating ideas or collecting pain points, list first and structure second. Use MECE to organize what you know and to avoid gaps in a diagnosis, not to constrain discovery.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Hidden risk.</strong> A neat MECE tree can look more certain than it is. Its branches are a claim about the problem's structure, and the claim can be wrong, which is why the next step tests the structure against facts.</p>\n<span id=\"c03-c03-3-issue-trees-building-pruning-prioritizing\"></span>\n<h4>C03.3 Issue trees: building, pruning, prioritizing</h4>\n<p>An <strong>issue tree</strong> is a hierarchy that breaks a question into MECE branches, down to items that can be tested. <a href=\"#gl-issue-tree\">issue tree</a> makes the structure visible to you and to the interviewer.</p>\n<p>Three kinds, defined by the root question:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Root question</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Diagnostic</td>\n<td>Why did X happen?</td>\n<td>Why did weekly retention drop?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Solution</td>\n<td>How can we achieve Y?</td>\n<td>How can we raise activation by 10%?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Decision</td>\n<td>Should we do Z?</td>\n<td>Should we build a CRM integration?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Building the tree</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Write the root question from the frame card.</li>\n<li>Choose the cut that serves the decision (C03.2).</li>\n<li>Split into three to five branches. Fewer than three means a missing branch unless the split is exhaustive by construction (new versus returning users, internal versus external causes); more than five means the cut is too fine for this level.</li>\n<li>Run the MECE tests (FW-C03-02).</li>\n<li>Split each branch the same way until the leaves are testable: each leaf names a check you could do with a specific data pull, interview, release log or experiment.</li>\n<li>Stop at two or three levels in an interview. Depth beyond that belongs to the branches you decide to investigate.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Pruning.</strong> Cross out branches that facts you already have rule out. The user told you the drop is sudden, so slow-moving causes such as gradual competitor gains drop down the list.</p>\n<p><strong>Prioritizing.</strong> A tree with twenty leaves is not an answer. Score each branch, or each leaf, on three things:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Likelihood:</strong> given what you know, how likely is it to explain the problem?</li>\n<li><strong>Impact if true:</strong> how much of the problem would it explain, and how much would fixing it matter?</li>\n<li><strong>Cost to check:</strong> how fast and cheap is the check?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Check first what is likely and cheap, then what is likely and expensive, and defer what is unlikely. The scoring is qualitative (low, medium, high) unless you have numbers. This is the <a href=\"#gl-pareto-principle\">Pareto principle</a> applied to analysis: a few branches usually explain most of the problem. The scored issue tree is FW-C03-03.</p>\n<p><strong>Limits.</strong> A tree is only as good as its cut. If the first branches all lead nowhere, step back and try a different cut rather than splitting further. A tree that is MECE but built along the wrong dimension produces confident dead ends.</p>\n<span id=\"c03-c03-4-hypothesis-driven-working-stating-testing-discarding\"></span>\n<h4>C03.4 Hypothesis-driven working: stating, testing, discarding</h4>\n<p>A <strong>hypothesis</strong> is a specific, testable statement about a cause or an effect that could turn out to be false. <a href=\"#gl-hypothesis\">hypothesis</a>-driven working means you form a guess early, say what would prove it wrong, and test the cheapest rejecting evidence first. It is faster than collecting data and waiting to see a pattern, because the hypothesis tells you which data to look at.</p>\n<p><strong>Anatomy of a useful hypothesis.</strong> A claim, a mechanism, a prediction and a rejection condition:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Claim: &quot;The drop is concentrated in Android users on version 5.2.&quot;</li>\n<li>Mechanism: &quot;The release changed how login tokens refresh.&quot;</li>\n<li>Prediction: &quot;Android sessions per user fell after the release date and iOS did not.&quot;</li>\n<li>Rejection: &quot;If iOS fell by a similar amount, or Android fell before the release, I reject it.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A guess like &quot;something is wrong with Android&quot; cannot be rejected, so it cannot be tested.</p>\n<p><strong>Hold several at once.</strong> One hypothesis invites anchoring. State two or three competing ones, and choose the check that best separates them. After each check, update: say which hypothesis the result lowers and which it raises, in a sentence.</p>\n<p><strong>Discard out loud.</strong> &quot;That rules out a data problem, because the dashboard and the raw logs agree.&quot; Interviewers listen for the discarding as much as for the hypothesis. Candidates who never discard are not testing.</p>\n<p><strong>Risks.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Confirmation bias.</em> You look for evidence for your favorite. The defense is to design the test that would reject it.</li>\n<li><em>Searching many cuts until something looks significant.</em> This produces false findings; each extra cut raises the chance that noise looks like a cause (multiple comparisons, C12).</li>\n<li><em>Premature hypotheses.</em> With no domain knowledge, a forced hypothesis is a guess. Explore descriptively first, then hypothesize.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The Build–Measure–Learn loop.</strong> The same logic applied to building: make the smallest thing that tests an assumption, measure it against a criterion set in advance, and decide what to do. It is FW-C03-05 and is extended for new products in C26.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to use it.</strong> When the test costs less than the debate, run the test: changing a label and watching a metric is cheaper than a meeting. And when you honestly have no basis for a hypothesis, a short descriptive pass (&quot;let me look at the breakdown by platform and week&quot;) is the better first move.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> A team is asked why a newly launched region shows low usage. They have no history, no benchmark and no prior launch to compare to. Spending an hour on hypotheses is guessing. A day of descriptive analysis (who signed up, where they stop, what they search for) produces the hypotheses worth testing. Hypothesis-driven working is a way to focus effort once there is something to focus on.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c03-c03-5-reusable-structures-for-product-problems\"></span>\n<h4>C03.5 Reusable structures for product problems</h4>\n";
